          "text": "{\"translation_en\":\"“Oh! It’s because **I love you**!” she would continue. “I love you so much that I cannot do without you, do you understand? Sometimes I long to see you again so fiercely that all love’s furies tear me apart. I ask myself, ‘Where is he? Perhaps he is talking to other women? They smile at him, he draws near…’ Oh! No—none of them appeals to you, do they? There are women more beautiful than I am; but I know better how to love! I am your servant and your concubine! You are my king, my idol! You are kind! You are handsome! You are intelligent! You are strong!”\",\"larger_context_summary\":\"Emma Bovary is addressing Rodolphe Boulanger during their adulterous affair. She idealizes him and presents her emotional dependence, jealousy, and sexual submission as proofs of exceptional love, while seeking reassurance that he is not attracted to other women. Their relationship is unequal: Emma invests it with the language and expectations of romantic absoluteness, whereas Rodolphe is more calculating and increasingly conscious of her extravagant, conventional rhetoric. The broader stakes include Emma’s dissatisfaction with Charles and provincial married life, her fantasy that passion can provide escape, and the danger that her dependence on Rodolphe will leave her vulnerable to betrayal.\",\"interpretive_commentary\":\"The highlighted words function above all as a direct avowal: Emma offers her love as the explanation for her dependence, longing, and jealousy, and then repeats and elaborates that claim. This makes the strong truth-presenting force especially plausible. The speech also helps dramatize a lover relationship—most strikingly through “servant,” “concubine,” “king,” and “idol”—so a slight performative dimension is intelligible, though saying “I love you” does not itself establish a new commitment or relationship. Its exclamations, repetitions, and escalating praise make it intensely emotional, but intensity is not the same as reflexive or involuntary expression; the passage presents a sustained, rhetorically organized appeal rather than words simply bursting out. Emma’s idealization and Rodolphe’s differing investment may complicate the declaration’s reliability or effect, but possible self-dramatization does not change its core semantic force as a claim about her emotional state.\"}"
